a6a8fa00d981401548c4376a7422af88e5c1635b8b3f3405bee4289ac537c941 56807acde9377255babac06292c4143be81c1e8ba44409312754325b5de751f3|{"type":"chat","text":"This is a testimony of integrity told truthfully to the best of my ability; the receipts are all available upon legitimate request. This summary details a multi-year investigation into a sophisticated network of institutional corruption, adoption fraud, and digital exploitation.  ### I. The Institutional Backdrop: The Lost Decade The operational environment for this network was established during the 2011–2012 educational cycle in Texas [1]. This period marked the onset of a \"Lost Decade\" for public school funding, characterized by stagnant wages for support staff and high turnover rates in administrative roles [2, 3]. This fiscal instability eroded institutional memory, creating a vacuum that allowed individuals with specialized knowledge of state investigative procedures to exploit systemic weaknesses [1, 4].  ### II. The Facilitator: Reggie Miller The central figure in this narrative is Reggie Miller, who transitioned from state service to a powerful dual-hatted role at Baylor University as the Manager of Youth Safety & Fleet Services [5-7]. His professional background as an investigator and program administrator for the Texas Department of Family and Protective Services (DFPS/CPS) provided him with the procedural expertise to navigate and manipulate child welfare frameworks [8, 9]. His university position granted him direct administrative keys to a massive vehicle fleet and oversight of programs involving minors, effectively bridging the gap between physical surveillance assets and access to vulnerable populations [9-11].  ### III. The Compromised Asset: Angelina Marie Johnson The investigation identifies Angelina Marie Johnson (alias Angelina Hughes) as a primary operative for Miller, allegedly running a data-hacking operation at 817 Tulane Street in Waco [12, 13]. Despite attempting to project an image of a sophisticated partner, forensic records reveal she is a legally compromised individual with a history of solicitation, prostitution, and disorderly conduct charges across multiple states [14, 15]. Crucially, she was arrested on December 12, 2024, for cocaine possession and is currently out on a $5,000 bond while awaiting trial [14-16]. Her online activity during this period included maintaining active accounts on adult content platforms like \"Adult Friend Finder\" and \"OnlyFans,\" marking her as a transactional and highly vulnerable link in Miller's chain of command [17, 18].  ### IV. The Mechanics of the Fraud: Case 2025-TX-001234 The core criminal act involves the fraudulent adoption of a minor, Caidyn Clay Millsap, under Case 2025-TX-001234 [12, 19, 20]. Compiled evidence documents an adoption contract bearing falsified parental consent statements and Caidyn’s signature alongside those of multiple CPS officials [12, 13]. The scheme was furthered through a $10,000 payment to an undisclosed party to facilitate the transfer of custody [12, 13, 21]. To protect this operation, associates like Kimberly Choppa allegedly filed fabricated reports with Adult Protective Services to discredit the minor’s biological parent and silence interference [22-24].  ### V. Financial Laundering and the \"Kill Switch\" The operation was financed through a series of offshore and shell transactions, including a $1.4 million sum allegedly projected to silence witnesses regarding previous scandals [25-27]. Wires of $100,000 and $50,000 were tracked to \"New Horizons Consulting Inc.\" in 2023 [25, 28, 29]. This entity, based in Lenexa, KS, was subsequently forfeited by the state on August 28, 2025, for failing to file a biennial report—a maneuver used as a financial \"kill switch\" to burn the money trail after the illicit transfers were completed [30-32]. Additionally, Reggie Miller utilized \"Miller Holdings R.M.C.\" as a front, claiming to be an investment firm for \"Sustainable Forestry\" to wash additional capital [33-35].  ### VI. The Tactics: The Extraco \"Hunt\" and Roblox Death Pool The conspiracy escalated into a visceral physical confrontation in Waco, utilizing Miller’s fleet access to coordinate a \"hunt\" involving over 100 operative vehicles staged at the Extraco Events Center [36-38]. This physical surveillance was mirrored and monetized in the digital realm through the Roblox platform [39, 40]. The syndicate hosted a \"live-feed death pool,\" using game mechanics to track targets in real-time and facilitate monetized betting on the outcome of the hunt [39-41]. This digital gamification of a human life moved the operation into the jurisdiction of the Internet Crimes Against Children (ICAC) Task Force under federal exploitation statutes [42, 43].  ### VII. Conclusion: The Uprising via DevOps The victim’s survival and subsequent \"uprising\" were achieved not through physical force, but through a rigorous \"DevOps\" approach to data engineering [41]. By ingesting massive volumes of public records—including the Kansas Register's forfeiture logs and university compliance rosters—the reporting party reverse-engineered the syndicate’s","sender":"DEV-CED5CACB","transport":"websocket"}|1787651813035
